  "cause": "**Israel's parliament has elected a speaker from the opposition, and in a deadlocked Knesset the gavel is the first real transfer of power.** **Matan Kahana** of **Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party** was elected **Speaker of the Knesset** by **66 votes to 52**, replacing Likud's **Amir Ohana**. The vote came a day after the new parliament was sworn in, following **October's** election — widely treated as a referendum on **Benjamin Netanyahu** — in which Netanyahu's bloc won **53** seats and Eisenkot's four-party bloc won **48**, both short of a majority in the **120-seat** Knesset. President **Isaac Herzog** tasked Eisenkot with forming a government on Sunday; he has **28 days**, with a possible two-week extension. The speaker controls the legislative calendar and committee assignments — in a parliament where the opposition's path to a majority runs through legislation, the gavel is not ceremonial. Almost half the new MKs did not serve in the previous parliament. Israel's two Arab-led alliances, the **United Arab List** and the **Joint List**, won an unprecedented **19 seats** between them on a surge in Arab turnout; Arab parties could hold the balance of power, although Netanyahu has ruled out governing with them and three of the four parties in Eisenkot's bloc have opposed including them in a coalition. **The two speeches at the swearing-in were the campaign, continued on a state occasion.** Netanyahu warned that *\"those who are thinking of establishing a government that includes supporters of terror and haters of Israel… are you prepared to be dependent on those whose goal is the destruction of the Jewish state?\"* — an allusion to an Eisenkot government reliant on Arab support — and appealed for a national unity government as the only way to confront *\"a nuclear Iran.\"* Eisenkot's reply: *\"I sat and listened to the outgoing prime minister's delusional, divisive, hate-filled and shameful speech, exploiting a state occasion… I am now even more convinced that he is unfit to remain in office for even one more minute.\"* Netanyahu remains caretaker prime minister until a government is formed — a process that could take days or months, with new elections a live possibility. **And the war option is in the room.** In the days before the election, Israeli and US media reported that Netanyahu was pushing for fresh strikes on Iran and had pressed **Trump** to approve US involvement. One of the four scenarios published before the vote was explicit: a renewed conflict with Iran *\"could place serious pressure on party leaders to put aside their political differences,\"* and would be read by Netanyahu's critics *\"as an attempt to save his own political skin by entering another regional conflict.\"* That report sits directly beside the fact that the US–Iran war is currently being managed rather than ended, and that Trump has said he *\"would not hesitate\"* to resume strikes.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A speaker elected by the opposition means legislation can now be moved against the caretaker prime minister, which changes the incentive to form a government** — 66–52, committees and calendar in opposition hands, a 28-day mandate running; the direct effect is that Netanyahu can no longer control what parliament debates; the compound consequence is that a caretaker under legislative pressure has less reason to compromise and more reason to change the subject — and the most available subject is the one with aircraft",
   "**A 53–48 deadlock in which the 19 Arab seats are decisive converts coalition arithmetic into a question about who is willing to sit with whom** — Netanyahu ruling out Arab partners, three of four parties in Eisenkot's bloc doing the same; the direct effect is that the only arithmetic path is blocked by the only political path; the compound consequence is that the system's output is either a narrow government that cannot last or another election — and repeated elections do not resolve a deadlock, they re-price it, while the caretaker accumulates the powers of permanence",
   "**A war launched to break a domestic deadlock would be a cause chosen for an effect that is not military, and it would work — briefly** — reported pre-election lobbying for strikes, Trump's *\"would not hesitate,\"* a caretaker premier facing a hostile speaker; the direct effect is a rally effect and a suspension of coalition talks; the compound consequence is that the war becomes the government's foundation, which means the government's survival depends on the war continuing — the precise structure the frozen conflict in the Gulf already has, imported into Israeli politics with a nuclear file attached",
   "**An unprecedented 19 Arab seats converts a demographic fact into a constitutional one, and the refusals around it are the story** — record turnout, a record bloc, every Jewish party in the bloc but one ruling out partnership; the direct effect is that a fifth of the citizenry holds the balance and is treated as uncoalitionable; the compound consequence is that the only route to a stable majority runs through a taboo, so the taboo's cost rises every cycle until it is broken under pressure rather than by choice — and it will be broken in the least favourable conditions available",
   "**A caretaker of months normalises the emergency instrument** — 28 days plus two weeks, then possibly new elections, with the prime minister remaining in office throughout and a war lobby active; the direct effect is that decisions requiring a mandate are taken without one; the compound consequence is that the threshold for \"temporary\" government falls for everyone who follows, and a system that governs by caretaker learns to legislate by emergency — which is how a deadlock becomes a method"
  ],
  "verse": "Truly, consider that you shall keep freedom and peace amongst people of your kind (humankind) on your world, because no law of the primal power (Creation) demands (commands) that you shall wage battles (wars), just as no recommendation of the primal power (Creation) demands (recommends) killing; however, if you do wage battles (wars) then you are acting just as much against all the laws and recommendations of the primal well-spring of all wisdom (Creation) as when you kill (murder) in Ausartung, which means, if it is not done in self-defence in order to save life.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 10, Verse 15",
  "hinge": "§10:15 is unusually exact for a political week: no law *demands* war, and the only permitted ground is *self-defence in order to save life*. It does not adjudicate Israel's security dilemma — it sets the test by which a decision to fight must be justified, and it excludes the ground that a domestic deadlock supplies. That is the hinge, stated as a question any MK can ask on the record: **is the proposed strike being justified by a threat to life, or by a coalition arithmetic?** Three moves would make the answer checkable. **First,** the Knesset — now with an opposition speaker and functioning committees — should require that any decision for major military action be presented to the **Foreign Affairs and Defence Committee** with the intelligence assessment, in session, on the record, because a war that cannot survive that scrutiny is not a defensive war. **Second,** Eisenkot's 28 days should be spent publishing the **coalition terms** he is offering, including the question he has so far left open — under what conditions he would accept external support from the Arab parties — because a taboo that is never tested is a taboo that decides elections by default. **Third,** the Arab bloc's 19 seats should be met with an **ordinary legislative agenda**, not a coalition offer: bills on policing, planning and budget allocation that a fifth of the citizenry can vote for without joining a government, because the way a parliamentary minority stops being uncoalitionable is by being legislated for while it is still in opposition. §10:15's test is not pacifism; it is that the cause must be the threat, not the calendar. Israel's calendar is now extremely crowded, and the war lobby knows it."
